From 6f00f01e0419a772bd5d6e548aab88984cfd1801 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: mpenelange <191051681+mpenelange@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sat, 10 Oct 2026 14:37:00 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 09/18] Fix use-after-free and torn reads between the fast path and flow learning

The rx fast path reads flow entries and their flow_info without the
bucket lock, while the learn path (another core) replaced entries and
kfree()d or memset() the flow_info in use. The entry
id0/id1 check didn't help: the reader only checked it before comparing
the key, then re-read flow_info later, and writers set flow_info outside
the id window with no memory barriers (Octeon is weakly ordered). A
reader could use freed memory, or match flow A and forward with flow B's
rewrite data.

- flow_info is freed with kfree_rcu (call_rcu + poison in IPFWD_POISON
  test builds); the rx hooks (pkt_receive, pkt_receive_cb) and the /proc
  cache dumps are RCU read-side sections
- the learn path never rewrites a published flow_info: it builds a new
  one, publishes it, and RCU-frees the old one
- id0/id1 is now a real sequence count: writers set id0, smp_wmb, change
  the entry (key and flow_info), smp_wmb, set id1; readers copy the entry
  between reading id1 and id0 and use only that consistent copy
- the /proc flush and resize handlers wait for a grace period
  (synchronize_net) instead of a fixed 20 us before freeing
- the /proc per-entry clear takes the bucket lock irqsave like the learn
  path (plain spin_lock could deadlock against the tx hook)

Tested on the ER-4 with a flow-churn stress test (8-bucket cache, 4x200 Mbit/s
UDP each way, 500 s of ~500k pps flow churn, then 50 pps churn). Poison
build without this fix: traffic died within seconds of the first phase
and the router stopped receiving on all ports (reboot needed). Poison
build with the fix: same load, 800/800 Mbit/s before and after the
flood, 0 kernel warnings, router reachable. Fast-path hits unchanged
(451k/s vs 450k/s).

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
